  "content_en": "Liu Ying, Emperor Hui of the Han Dynasty [Updated today] Regarding this chapter, it is just my personal opinion. It is said that a thousand readers have a thousand Hamlets, and everyone's interpretation of history is also different. Based on this interpretation, I wrote this novel. Naturally, I don’t think my interpretation is necessarily correct, but I think that as long as the concepts in the novel can be rounded out, it will be fine. Liu Ying, Emperor Hui of the Han Dynasty, reigned for seven years, at the end of 188 BC, when he was only twenty-four years old. After his death, the officials gave him the posthumous title \"Hui\". The word \"Hui\" means \"kindness and gentleness\". This posthumous title summed up his life. He was the eldest son of Liu Bang, the founding emperor of the Western Han Dynasty. He ascended the throne as emperor and his name was justified. He was the second emperor of the Han Dynasty, and his mother was Empress Lu, the first strange woman to hold great power in Chinese history. Emperor Hui is a strange blank area in the history of the Western Han Dynasty. Before him, there was Liu Bang, the founding emperor of the country with \"great talent and great strategy\". After that, there was the heroine who was in charge of power. After that, there was the rule of Wen and Jing. After that, there was the most glorious period in the history of the Western Han Dynasty, the Hanwu period. In the future, Zhaoxuan Zhongxing. After that...I won't die. In short, among this string of brilliant names, his name was obscured and dim. Even historians have weakened him intentionally or unintentionally. When Sima Qian wrote historical records, he did not even establish a family for him, and in his evaluation, he intentionally or unintentionally used the adjective \"benevolent and weak\", which does not seem to be a compliment. Although there is an independent chapter \"Huidi Ji\" in the Han Dynasty, the deeds are simple. In the end, he was called a merciful master, but he was the most virtuous at the expense of Empress Dowager Lu. I just think he's a nice guy. He is just very sad that his fatal opponent is his mother. Liu Ying was not a prince who grew up in fine clothes and fine food. When he was born, Liu Bang had not yet become too wealthy. He lived with his mother and sister in his hometown of Fengfei. When his mother and sister were busy with farm work, they put him on the edge of the field. The baby who was once placed on the edge of the field later became the king of a country. Destiny is reversed and often very strange. Liu Ying is not a prince who is ignorant of the ups and downs of the world. During the Chu-Han War, Liu Bang was at a disadvantage for a long time. In the second year of the Han Dynasty, during the Chu-Han War, Xiang Yu sent people to Fengpei to retrieve Liu Bang's family members. Six-year-old Liu Ying and her sister were fleeing when they met their father by chance and got on his father's carriage. Then, as the pursuers gradually approached, Liu Bang kicked them off the speeding carriage in order to survive. Fortunately, he was protected by his loyal subordinates and could not fall into the hands of the enemy in the end. But it was a life-or-death relationship at that time. His father is a scoundrel without much conscience. Her mother was a resolute, ruthless and perverted woman. However, the son they gave birth to miraculously had a kind soul. For any imperial family that started from humble beginnings, their feelings are mostly sincere at the beginning. Before Li Yuan raised his army, the three Li brothers had their time as brothers and brothers, but in the end it led to the tragedy of Xuanwu Sect. Fortunately, Liu Ying is the only legitimate son. His mother's family has played a lot of credit in Liu Bang's ascension to the throne. His mother is the mistress recognized by the ministers. As such, his throne seems to be as stable as Mount Tai. But he can't stand up to a father's partiality. After several years of fighting, my mother's lust faded and her love for Chi began to fade away. As he grew older, his father fell in love with a young concubine. Qi Yi. Qi Yi had a son as he wished, and he was said to be fond of his young son when he was old. My father has gone too far in his old age, and he loves Ruyi so much that he actually has thoughts about Yi Chu Yu Ruyi. Liu Ying was naturally unwilling to give in. But his unwillingness pales in comparison to his mother's. Because the mother has more reasons to be angry. I am your legitimate wife. I have taken care of your food, drink, and transportation for many years. I have taken care of your house, given birth to your children, been worried about you, and suffered all kinds of hardships for you. In the end, I got what I deserved, but you actually want to give it all to that little goblin who has never done anything and is enjoying the rewards. It was actually a war between women. Qi Yi is not smart. She only relies on Liu Bang's favor, but even an emperor cannot do everything as he pleases. As far as the ministers who conquered the world together with Liu Bang are concerned, they would certainly prefer to have a prince who has gone through hardships with them, rather than a child who is born in peace and is far away from them and not close to them. The prince can at least see kindness, but Ruyi is just a child with no final shape. Empress Lu was a legitimate mistress who shared hardships, but what was Qi Yi? She was just a concubine Liu Bang got on the roadside and was raised in the palace.dodder flower. The prince has grown up and can handle things independently. If the support goes well, Qi Yi will inevitably have to take care of him in the future. They are men who have been through fire and water, how can they be willing to bend down and obey orders from Qi Yi who has never experienced anything and only invites favors in the harem? Under the influence of his ministers and the people, Liu Bang had no choice but to give in. Then he had to dance and sing with his beauty. At that time, Liu Bang had already seen Qi Yi's final outcome. When I was young, I heard people talk about historians with great admiration. It is said that after an emperor rebelled, he summoned the historian and asked him how he would record the incident. The official historian stated straightforwardly that on a certain day of a certain year and a certain month, someone conspired to rebel. The emperor was furious and killed the historian. and called his brother. This younger brother, the historian, still wrote that someone was plotting rebellion. The emperor was very angry and killed him again. He killed several historians in succession and asked about the last historian. The historian still wrote verbatim: On a certain day of a certain month of a certain year, someone rebelled. In the end, the emperor had no choice. I can only wipe my nose, forget it. At that time, I had a very high respect for history. But the older I get, the more I realize that's not the case. Perhaps the historian cannot turn something black into white, but the historian can completely omit something, omit something, write something in detail, and publish a series of comments to guide readers' judgment.尤其是官修的史书，当书写本朝史时，是非常注意文过饰非的。 Even if there is no consideration for exaggeration, the political stance and attitude of the historian will lead him to favor or reject this historical figure and historical event, and this will be reflected in his writing. In this way, when we face history, how can we believe that it is the real history? Looking at other girls' interpretations of this period of history, it is written in \"It's Better Not to Encounter the Beauty of the City\": I like Emperor Hui's close protection of Ruyi, and the little favor that he couldn't bear to wake up his brother that morning. \"Even if the country is yours but you can't move it, you can't bear to look at it. You are stepping on the blood of your relatives every step of the way. Why don't you just settle on this blue bridge?\" Come on, lean on the railing and watch. Just throw this land made with the blood of your loved ones to the woman who wants it, and give her everything she wants. This is your own salvation.\" The girl burst out laughing, deconstructing history very gently. Even if it is as absurd as King You of Zhou, you can still see a little bit of sincerity in the brightening background of the beacon fire drama. As an emperor, this is naturally unqualified. So Sima Guang accused Emperor Hui: \"Abandoning the country without showing mercy...it can be said that he is devoted to small benevolence but does not know the great friendship.\" (Volume 12 of \"Zi Zhi Tong Jian\".) However, in fact, I also like this little bit of favor. Because no matter what, there are already too many emperors who know great friendship and have given up on Xiaoren, so emperors who can be loyal to Xiaoren are also very precious. Closer to the world of fireworks.每个人都知道，大谊重于小仁，可是，站在凡夫俗子的立场上说，我觉得，把握住更实在的小仁，作为一个人，会更幸福一点。 As far as Emperor Hui's dynasty was concerned, Sima Qian only wrote the chronicles of Empress Lu and did not write a separate biography for Emperor Hui. Among them, there were very few things about Emperor Hui. Among them, Emperor Hui was quoted as saying that Emperor Hui was \"benevolent and weak, not like me.\" It was also recorded that after Emperor Hui watched people's pigs, \"he burst into tears because he was ill and could not get up in his later years. He sent someone to invite the Queen Mother and said: 'This is not done by humans. I am the Queen Mother's son, and I will never be able to rule the world.' Xiao Hui Drinking on this day is a form of obscene pleasure, and one does not listen to the government, so one is sick. \"The last praise is that during the time of Emperor Xiaohui and Gaohou, the people were freed from the sufferings of the Warring States period, and the emperor and ministers all wanted to rest and do nothing. Therefore, Emperor Hui bowed his head, and Gaohou was the heroine. The government did not leave the house, and the world was peaceful. Punishment is rarely used, but sinners are lucky. Civil affairs include farming, food and clothing, and breeding. \"Historical Records\" has a very high status in the history books. Therefore, it greatly affected future generations' impression of Emperor Hui. Although Queen Lu's pigs were tragic, for an emperor, he was discouraged because of it. He \"drank daily for sexual pleasure and did not listen to politics.\" It is not something worthy of praise. Fortunately, Sima Qian finally gave a favorable evaluation, although it was only incidental. \"Book of Han\" Ban Gu had a pity attitude toward Emperor Hui, praising: \"Hui cultivates filial piety internally and is courteous to the prime minister externally. He favors Qi Mou and Zhao Yin favorably, showing kindness and respect.\" Hearing Shusun Tong's advice, he was frightened. He accepted the advice of Prime Minister Cao and said it sincerely. He can be said to be a tolerant master. Empress Dowager Cao Lu suffered a loss of great virtue, she is sad for her husband! The further we go to later generations, the further away we are from the Han Dynasty, the events at that time become more distant and out of reach, and the truth becomes more and more elusive. The Han Dynasty was overthrown, the Liu family was no longer honored, people's fear of Liu Han faded away, and people became more and more rude in their speech.Come. Just reading the account of Emperor Hui watching people pig in the Historical Records, we get the impression that Emperor Hui gave up on himself and did nothing after suffering major psychological trauma. According to later historical records, Sima Guang accused Emperor Hui of \"abandoning the country without caring... he was devoted to small benevolence but did not know the great friendship\" (Volume 12 of \"Zi Zhi Tong Jian\"). Some people also accused him of \"ignoring the country, being a mediocre leader of the Han family, and a rebellious son of Emperor Gao Zu\" (\"On Empress Lu's Dictatorship and the Zhu Lu Incident\", \"Journal of Chengchi University\" No. 20, 1969.) Some scholars also believe that this is the reason why Sima Qian did not set a record for him. But did Emperor Hui really indulge in obscene pleasures and ignore government affairs from then on? Also according to historical records, before the death of Prime Minister Xiao He in July of the following year, Emperor Hui visited him in person and asked about his successor. This shows that he did not stop asking about political affairs after watching people pigging. Later, Cao Shen drank day and night without asking about government affairs. Emperor Hui also scolded him and said: \"You are the prime minister, drinking every day and asking for nothing, why do you worry about the world?\" How does this look like he is ignoring government affairs? I respect Sima Qian, but the more I read historical records, the more I feel that this is actually a documentary novel, is it true...? . Similarly, regarding Empress Lu's desire to give King Qi fat and poisonous wine, according to historical records, Emperor Hui did not know that what he was holding was poisonous wine, but insisted on treating King Qi with \"family courtesy\", and the meaning of disobedience to his mother was not obvious. However, according to Liu Xiang's records, Emperor Hui knew that Empress Lu wanted to poison Queen Qi to death and she insisted on drinking poisoned wine for King Qi in order to remonstrate to death, so Empress Lu gave up. (Note: \"New Preface: Shanmou Xia\". Liu Xiang is a member of the royal family, the fourth generation grandson of King Chu Yuan. He was born about forty or fifty years later than Sima Qian.) Regarding the Liu family's family affairs, I always feel that Liu Xiang's records are more credible. sigh. Moreover, \"Cui Gong\" does not mean \"not listening to politics\", it does not mean \"disrespecting the country\", nor does it mean \"abandoning the country without caring\", but \"governing by doing nothing\". In the early Han Dynasty, when the people had ten houses and nine empty houses, and people's livelihood was in decline, Emperor Gaozu and later Emperor Hui, Empress Lu, Emperor Wen, and Emperor Jing all used Huang Lao to govern the country and rule by doing nothing. Why were they praising others, but when it came to Emperor Hui, they were criticizing. The battle between Liu and Lu in the early years of the Western Han Dynasty was extremely tragic. Many people died in the two most noble surnames of the Han Dynasty. After the death of Empress Lu, the Lu family was virtually exterminated. However, these internal battles within the royal family had little impact on the people's livelihood. The national power of the Han Dynasty gradually developed through the persistence of several generations of emperors, and finally ushered in the peak era of Emperor Wu. Emperor Hui never abandoned his country, and his presence had a great impact on Empress Lu. At least, when Emperor Hui was around, apart from her mother's two brothers, Queen Lu could not win more marquis positions for the Lu family. If Emperor Hui really didn't care about state affairs and the state affairs were in the hands of Empress Lu, would Empress Lu have to wait until Emperor Hui's death before she could ennoble the princes with the surname Lu? In the early years of the Han Dynasty, the emphasis was on the family and the world. In terms of state affairs, Emperor Hui was the legitimate emperor, and he was an adult, so Empress Lu could not exert too much control. However, in terms of family affairs, Empress Lu, as a mother, had great authority. After all, Liu Ying was a very filial person, and he did not rise from the position of prince to emperor without any hardships. He had experienced a lot of time with his mother and sister among the people, and he had experienced the life-and-death escape in the Chu-Han War. As a mother, Empress Lu never felt sorry for him, even her mother-in-law. My mother suffered too much in the war, and after becoming the empress, she also spent too much effort in order to keep her throne. Judging from Emperor Hui's close protection of his half-brother who threatened him, how could he not be filial and loving to his mother? This is the reason why Emperor Hui was controlled by Empress Lu. Dealing with Mrs. Qi is a family matter, and it is a mistress who deals with concubines. King Ruyi of Zhao and King Liu Fei of Qi were the concubines of Empress Lu. When Emperor Hui married a wife, he had to obey his parents' orders. All these were considered family affairs. …What state affairs did Empress Yalu handle during Emperor Hui’s reign? Turn left, turn right, turn rice out. So, was Emperor Hui a qualified emperor? I think the answer is yes. To say the least, he had the ability to act as a qualified emperor in his time. In the early Han Dynasty when people's livelihood was in decline, it was appropriate to adopt a national policy of recuperation and recuperation. Is this now recognized by historians? In this era background, a qualified emperor needs to be tolerant and able to listen to the opinions of his subordinates, or he may also have the ability to discover talents. Of these three conditions, except for the third one which was not fully reflected because Emperor Hui's reign was too short, I think Emperor Hui did a good job in the first two. Emperor Wen and Emperor Jing were able to produce a literary workJing Zhizhi, why can't Liu Ying do it? At least, if Emperor Wu of the Han Dynasty was thrown to this time in the early Han Dynasty, I estimate that it would be a disaster for the country and the people. The national strength accumulated by several generations of emperors was wasted by him. Otherwise, why would there be Zhaoxuan Zhongxing later? If ZTE is needed, it means that the national power of the Western Han Dynasty before Zhaoxuan was defeated by Emperor Wu, leaving his son and great-grandson to clean up the mess. Emperor Hui's life was too short, and his conflict with his mother was too deep. In fact, what did Queen Lu want? Wu Zetian killed her son. At least she once lived up to her position of supreme status and lived her life in vain. And it was her descendants who finally restored the Tang Dynasty. Where is Queen Lu? She was determined to fight for the Lu family and killed so many people from the Liu family. In the end, she died and almost all of the Lu family was destroyed. Even her grandsons were all killed on the grounds that they were not the sons of Emperor Hui. Zhang Yan died in Beigong. Zhang Yan, the ending seems not to be good either. If this is the case, it can almost be said that after her death, all the descendants of her natal family, the Lu family, who inherited her bloodline, died. Heaven and earth are long. Chinese people are most particular about three types of unfilial piety, the greatest of which is not having children. Women, in particular, are more attached to their children than men. Is this what Empress Lu wants? The world is long, if she has a soul, can she still see traces of her own in the world? The price is too heavy. I think it's not worth it. I wonder if Empress Lu knew something secretly, would she regret it? Or, just for those eight years of being the king behind the scenes who had all the power? Or, are there really so many conflicts between the husband's family and her natal family that they can't let go? The most important thing in dealing with others is to stand on the right side. As a mother, the most important thing to Empress Lu should be the well-being of her children. Which is more important, the mother's family or the son? Most people will choose the son. I don't know how Queen Lu chooses. Anyway, as far as I am concerned, in a mother's heart, there are only two things that can compete with her son for status. One is justice and truth, and the other is herself. Justice and truth are out of the question. If she wants to fight, she can make sense for herself. So what is most important to you? Well, besides yourself, it is your son and your natal family. Dizzy, I went back again. Who is more important, the son or the natal family? son. So, why was there such a quarrel with Emperor Hui? When you become a queen or even a queen mother, you naturally have to consider your natal family. It is understandable to give honor to your natal family to a certain extent. But this certain level must always be mastered. In the end, the mother's family is stronger than her son, so what on earth is going on? Or, after Emperor Hui's death, the young emperor was young and had to do whatever he wanted to do, ascend the throne himself or change the dynasty to the Lu family. But when he ascends the throne, it is his descendants who have to pass it on. And it was given to my natal family. Have you ever seen any ancestral hall where your aunt is enshrined? This is definitely a paradox. The fierce dispute between Liu and Lu in the early Western Han Dynasty was largely a matter of Empress Lu's position. Empress Lu may have done something wrong, but what about Emperor Hui? What did he do wrong that warrants the punishment of extirpation? This result is too cruel. In fact, after Empress Lu's death, there were no more Liu Ying members of the royal family named Liu. After Emperor Wen, historians compiled history and found that Emperor Gao was the common ancestor, and Empress Lu was considered Emperor Wen's legitimate mother after all, so she could not be trampled upon openly, but what about Emperor Hui. He was one of the people left out of the emperor's lineage. A lone branch. Overseas orphans. Is it worth it? He punished himself and abused himself. At the young age of twenty-four, his body was ruined by drinking and having fun, and he fell into the court. As long as you are alive, people will care about your face. When you die, there is nothing left. The direct line of Emperor Gaozu was cut off from then on. Thousands of years later, I checked the Liu family genealogy information on the Internet, and it was written that Emperor Hui had mediocre talents.",
  "translated": true
}